# Eitri's keys

This tutorial teaches the keys of the Eitri window around this editor: the
prefix, the modules, the agent panel, the bottom terminal, floats and
workspaces. It takes about fifteen minutes if you do every step.

Neovim's own keys work here as always. Move down this page with `j`{normal}.
What you type into this page is never written to disk.

# Lesson 0

Most steps in this tutorial happen outside nvim, in the window: each one says
what you should see and how to come back here. One way back always works:
`{prefix} e` gives the keys to this editor from any other module. (Pressed
while the editor already has the keys, it hides the editor instead; press it
again to bring it back.)

The keys are written with `{prefix}` as the prefix, the one Eitri has now (the
default prefix is `Ctrl+b`). Lesson 7 says where another one comes from. This
tutorial teaches the stock keys after the prefix: if your tmux config rebinds
some of them, `{prefix} ?` shows what they do here.

When a line has a ✗ at its left, you fix it. Most of these lines ask for a
key: press `A`{normal}, type a space and the key as this tutorial spells it
(for a chord, its letters, such as Ctrl+l), then press `<Esc>`{normal}. The ✗
turns into ✓ when the line is right. Try it on the line below, which wants a
`?`:

Press the prefix, then this key, to list every key: {prefix}

# Lesson 1: THE PREFIX

** Press {prefix}, let go, then press one more key. **

The window's own keys start with a prefix, as in tmux. Eitri reads it before
the editor, the agent panel or the terminal sees the key, so it works wherever
the keys are.

 1. Press `{prefix}` and let go. The top bar shows a strip of what can follow,
    ending with `? keys`.

 2. Press `<Esc>`. The strip goes away and nothing else happens. A key the
    prefix does not bind also ends the wait without doing anything.

 3. Press `{prefix} ?`. The agent panel shows the full list of keys, starting
    at the ones that follow the prefix. Your own changes and anything taken
    from tmux are in it. Close it with `q` or `<Esc>`: the keys come back here.

NOTE: The keys in this tutorial are the most used ones. `{prefix} ?` always
      shows all of them, and the hint strip shows what may follow the prefix.

# Lesson 2: SPLITS, ZOOM AND CLOSING A MODULE

** {prefix} % opens a module to the right, {prefix} " below. **

The window is made of modules: this editor, the agent panel and the bottom
terminal. A split key waits for a second key that names the module: `e` for
the editor, `a` for the agent panel, `t` for the terminal. If the terminal is
already open, the split moves it there.

NOTE: Steps 3 and 4 close the bottom terminal, which ends its shell and all
      that runs in it, also when the terminal was hidden. If it runs
      something you need, such as a build, a server or an agent, leave out
      the closing in steps 3 and 4: press `{prefix} e` to come back here.

 1. Press `{prefix} %`, then `t`. The terminal opens to the right of the
    editor and takes the keys.

 2. Press `{prefix} z`. The terminal fills the window. Press `{prefix} z`
    again and it goes back to its place.

 3. Press `{prefix} x`. The top bar asks whether to kill the pane. Press `y`:
    the terminal's shell ends and the terminal closes. The keys go to a
    neighbouring module; if that is not this editor, press `{prefix} e`.

 4. Press `{prefix} "`, then `t`. This time the terminal opens below the
    editor. Close it again with `{prefix} x` and `y`.

NOTE: Do not press `{prefix} x` while this editor has the keys. It ends nvim,
      and with it this tutorial, and the editor does not come back until you
      open a new Eitri window; with the editor as the last module on screen,
      it closes Eitri.

 5. Fix the line below:

Zoom this module, or restore it: {prefix}

# Lesson 3: MOVING WITH CTRL+H, CTRL+J, CTRL+K, CTRL+L

** Ctrl+h, Ctrl+j, Ctrl+k and Ctrl+l move the keys left, down, up and
   right. **

These four need no prefix. In the agent panel, `Ctrl+j` starts typing in
BROWSE and leaves downward from the message box, and `Ctrl+k` returns from the
box to BROWSE and leaves upward from BROWSE (the modes are in lesson 4). In
nvim they move between nvim's own windows first and leave for the next module
only when there is no nvim window that way, as vim-tmux-navigator does in tmux.

 1. Press `Ctrl+l`. The keys go to the agent panel on the right.

 2. Press `Ctrl+h`. The keys come back to this editor.

 3. Type `:vsplit`{vim} and press `<Enter>`. Now `Ctrl+l` first moves to the
    right half of nvim, and only a second `Ctrl+l` reaches the panel. Press
    `Ctrl+h` until you are back in the left half, then type
    `:only`{vim}.

 4. A few more ways to move the keys:
    `{prefix} o`      the next module on screen
    `{prefix} ;`      the module that had the keys before
    `{prefix} Left`   the module on that side (also Right, Up and Down)
    `{prefix} f`      labels the modules and buttons you can jump to; type a
                    label to go there

NOTE: If your nvim config maps `Ctrl+h`, `Ctrl+j`, `Ctrl+k` or `Ctrl+l` to
      something else, your mapping wins and Eitri leaves that key alone. Use
      the arrows after the prefix then.

 5. Fix the line below:

Go back to the module that had the keys before: {prefix}

# Lesson 4: THE AGENT PANEL

** The panel has two modes: INSERT is the message box, BROWSE is the
   conversation. **

The agent panel is where you talk to an agent. In BROWSE its keys are vim's.
The left end of its bottom band shows the mode, the way your statusline shows
nvim's.

 1. Press `Ctrl+l` to go to the panel. If the band says BROWSE, press `i`.

 2. Type a few words, then press `Ctrl+u` to delete them. Do not press
    `<Enter>` in this lesson: it would send what you typed to the agent and
    start a turn.

 3. Press `<Esc>`. The band says BROWSE. Here `j` and `k` move over the rows of
    the conversation, `F1` lists the panel's keys (`q` closes the list), and
    `i` goes back to the box.

 4. Each conversation is a tab. Press `{prefix} c`: a new, empty tab opens
    and waits for its first message. `{prefix} n` and `{prefix} p` go to the
    next and the previous tab, `{prefix} 1` to the first. `{prefix} &`
    closes the tab you are on after a `y`. Close the new one that way: go
    back to it (`{prefix} n` until you are on it), then press `{prefix} &`
    and `y`. In BROWSE, `H` and `L` also step through the tabs.

 5. When an agent wants to run a tool that needs your approval, a card waits
    in the conversation. There is none now, so read this one:
    `Alt+n` takes the next waiting card into card mode (from the box too),
    and so does `<Enter>` on the card's row in BROWSE. Then `1` approves, `2`
    denies, and `3` approves and saves a rule where the card offers one.
    `Tab` opens a box for a reason (in Claude tabs; Codex and ACP agents take
    no reason). `<Esc>` leaves card mode with the card still waiting, and
    `Alt+p` goes to the previous card. Letters never answer a card, and a
    digit answers only once card mode has settled and no other key follows
    it, so a word you are typing cannot answer a card by accident.

 6. Press `Ctrl+h` to come back here.

 7. Fix the line below:

Open a new tab in the agent panel: {prefix}

# Lesson 5: THE BOTTOM TERMINAL, AND THE PREFIX TWICE

** {prefix} t shows the terminal; {prefix} {prefix} sends {prefix}
   itself. **

In the terminal, the prefix and the four moving keys belong to Eitri, so the
shell never sees them. The prefix gives them back.

 1. Press `{prefix} t`. The terminal opens below the editor with the keys. Its
    shell starts in the project's directory.

 2. Type `ls` and press `<Enter>`. To clear the screen, `Ctrl+l` will not do:
    it moves the keys. Press `{prefix} Ctrl+l` instead, which sends Ctrl+l to
    the shell. `{prefix} Ctrl+h`, `{prefix} Ctrl+j` and `{prefix} Ctrl+k`
    do the same for those keys, such as readline's Ctrl+k, which deletes to
    the end of the line.

 3. Press `{prefix} [` to scroll back through the terminal's output. `k` and
    `j` move a line, `q` leaves.

 4. Press `{prefix} {prefix}` to send {prefix} to the program. For Ctrl+b,
    a shell moves the cursor back one character, and Claude Code running in
    this terminal takes it as the key that moves a running command to the
    background.

 5. Press `Ctrl+k`. The keys come back here, to the editor above. With the
    keys in the terminal, `{prefix} t` hides it again.

 6. The same goes for nvim, where Ctrl+b scrolls a page back. Press
    `<C-f>`{normal} to go a page forward, then `{prefix} {prefix}`: nvim
    gets {prefix}; for Ctrl+b that goes a page back to this lesson.

 7. Fix the line below:

Clear the terminal's screen: {prefix}

# Lesson 6: FLOATS, THE SCRATCHPAD AND WORKSPACES

** A float sits above the other modules, the scratchpad keeps floats out of
   sight, and a workspace keeps a whole arrangement under a name. **

 1. Press `{prefix} t` to show the terminal and give it the keys.

 2. Press `{prefix} F`. The terminal floats in the middle of the window,
    above the others. The editor never floats.

 3. Press `{prefix} _`. The float goes to the scratchpad, out of sight. Its
    name in the top bar now ends with `⌁`, and its shell keeps running.

 4. Press `{prefix} S`. It comes back. Press it again and it goes away;
    once more brings it back.

 5. With the keys on the float, press `{prefix} F` to put it
    back among the other modules. Then press `{prefix} e` to come back here.

The Alt chords after the prefix do the same: Alt+Shift+Space for
`{prefix} F`, Alt+_ for `{prefix} _`, and Alt+- for `{prefix} S`.

 6. Press `{prefix} s`. The hint strip lists the workspaces and ends with
    `c new · x delete`. Press `c`: Eitri makes a new workspace, a copy of
    this arrangement named with the lowest free number from 2, and takes you
    there. Press `{prefix} z` to zoom the editor.

 7. Press `{prefix} L`. You are back in the workspace you started in,
    unzoomed, as you left it. To delete the copy, press `{prefix} s`, then
    `x`, then the digit the strip shows beside the copy; it goes at once,
    without a question. Every workspace holds the same modules; only their
    arrangement differs.

 8. Fix the line below:

Float this module, or put a float back: {prefix}

# Lesson 7: YOUR TMUX CONFIG, AND INIT.LUA

** Eitri takes your tmux prefix and bindings; init.lua changes the rest. **

If you use tmux, Eitri read your tmux config when it started (the files tmux
reads, such as ~/.tmux.conf or ~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf). Your prefix and the
bindings that match an Eitri action work here too. A notice says what came
across, once for each change, and `{prefix} ?` lists every line that did not,
with the reason. Eitri only reads those files; it never starts or asks tmux.

Your own changes go in ~/.config/eitri/init.lua (with $XDG_CONFIG_HOME set,
in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eitri/init.lua). Eitri reads it when it starts, and what
it says wins over what came from tmux. The calls look like this:

~~~ lua
local k = eitri.keymap
k.prefix("C-a")                   -- the prefix becomes Ctrl+a
k.del("prefix", "C-b")            -- Ctrl+b after the prefix sent Ctrl+b
k.set("prefix", "C-a", "send-prefix")  -- now Ctrl+a twice sends Ctrl+a
~~~

A key that already has an action must be deleted with `k.del` before `k.set`
gives it another; otherwise Eitri stops at startup and says which call to fix.

 1. The sample line below sets a prefix to Ctrl+b. Change it to Ctrl+a: move
    the cursor onto the `b` and press `ra`{normal}.

eitri.keymap.prefix("C-b")

 2. To turn the tmux import off, init.lua says:

    eitri.config.set("keymap.from_tmux", "off")

Two samples come with Eitri's source, in docs/keymap/: tmux-ctrl-a.lua moves
the prefix to Ctrl+a with the rest of a common tmux setup, and i3-alt-mod.lua
is for i3 or sway with Alt as $mod. Such a window manager takes the Alt chords
before Eitri sees them (`{prefix} Alt+w` and the others), and the sample moves
them to Ctrl+Alt chords: with it, the alias {prefix} Alt+Shift+Space becomes
{prefix} Ctrl+Alt+Space. Lesson 6's plain keys need no such change.

# THE END

These are the keys from this tutorial:

    `{prefix} ?`                  every key
    `{prefix} %`, `{prefix} "`    then e, a or t: open that module right of,
                                or below, this one
    `{prefix} z`                  zoom, or restore
    `{prefix} x`                  close this module, after a y
    `Ctrl+h` `Ctrl+j` `Ctrl+k` `Ctrl+l`   move the keys
    `{prefix} e`, `{prefix} a`, `{prefix} t` editor, agent panel, terminal
                                (pressed there: hide it)
    `{prefix} c`, `{prefix} n`, `{prefix} p`    new, next, previous tab
    `{prefix} {prefix}`          send {prefix} to the module with the keys
    `{prefix} Ctrl+l`             send Ctrl+l (clear a shell's screen)
    `{prefix} F`                 float, or put back
    `{prefix} _`, `{prefix} S`    to the scratchpad, and back
    `{prefix} s`, `{prefix} L`      choose a workspace, the last one

The guide on https://eitri.cc/docs/ has every key and setting. Run
`:EitriTutor`{vim} to start this tutorial again.

